      "writtenOn": "2020-05-05T21:43:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wonder if this is the right abstraction. For stubs it\u0027s somewhat incidental that they apply to device only; in reality it\u0027s a global attribute that only is applicable for certain builds, i.e. those that pay heed to the apex dimension.\n\nWhat we would like to be able to is to say that some property always has to be defined globally, so the common value optimisation has to succeed fully. For a particular variant the value can be either X or N/A, and over all variants where it isn\u0027t N/A it has to have the same value X, or else it\u0027s a panic (because it should have come from the same property in the source module). It probably works well enough to use the Zero value as N/A here.\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "f95eeaa108c72375500d4f9b7d71000ad3d85c23",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d022a698_d7696866",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-06T09:03:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So basically mark properties that are \"not-arch-variant\". Or maybe just require properties that are arch specific to be marked as such and assume if not marked they are not arch specific. That will match the properties structures used by the modules and make it easier to understand (and possibly allow sharing of structures).\n\nI like your suggestion about failing if we detect that a non-arch variant property has arch specific values as it would detect any issues early on and would avoid situations where we don\u0027t find out until the snapshot is unpacked that there was a problem.\n\nHowever, I think it is orthogonal to this mechanism. This mechanism requires positive confirmation (on host) that it is ok to ignore the X value (which may be significant in some cases).",
      "parentUuid": "687f1202_7882cf26",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "f95eeaa108c72375500d4f9b7d71000ad3d85c23",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "adfb310f_9e5e2454",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-06T11:34:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It wouldn\u0027t ignore an X value, it would only ignore an N/A value. The logic to not propagate some non-X and non-N/A value for host (if necessary) would instead be in PopulateFromVariant, and hence no need for this tag.\n\n\u003e (which may be significant in some cases)\n\nHow do you mean? If it\u0027s significant then it by definition shouldn\u0027t be ignored, by tag or other means, so we either need to support the property being an arch-variant, or fail. This is not the case for stubs at least.",
      "parentUuid": "d022a698_d7696866",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "f95eeaa108c72375500d4f9b7d71000ad3d85c23",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "971fe1dd_072497f5",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-07T15:11:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry, I meant would ignore a significant N/A value. In the rest of this I am going to assume N/A is represented by Zero value.\n\nIf the only criteria we have for ignoring an N/A value is that the property is not marked as `arch_variant` then we have no way of identifying a \u0027significant\u0027 N/A value. It is possible that we could ignore an N/A value that is significant which could cause problems.\n\nSo, despite the stubs.versions property not being marked as `arch_variant`, the StubsVersion() method returns different values for host than it does for android.\n\nLets, say that for some reason one Android variant ends up with a different value to the rest.\n\nSo, we have:\nandroid variant1: StubsVersion() -\u003e \"1\"\nandroid variant2: StubsVersion() -\u003e N/A\nandroid variant3: StubsVersion() -\u003e \"1\"\n\nMy change will detect the inconsistency and fail because the N/A value in StubsVersion() will only be ignored on host variants.\n\nSimilarly, if a host variant ends up with a non-N/A value for some reason, e.g.\nhost variant1: StubsVersion() -\u003e \"1\"\nhost variant2: StubsVersion() -\u003e N/A\n\nThen my change will detect that because it only ignores N/A values on host variants.\n\nObviously, this is not something that is likely with StubsVersion() but there are/will be many properties over which we have little control.\n\nIt is all about protecting this code against incompatible changes in other parts of Soong. I\u0027d rather detect the issue as early as possible.",
